==Series IIA Forward Control==
[[File:1963 Land Rover 112 Recovery Wagon Heritage Motor Centre, Gaydon.jpg|thumb|right|1963 Land Rover Forward Control Recovery Wagon]]
The '''Series IIA FC''' launched in 1962 was based on the Series IIA 2.25-litre petrol engine and {{convert|109|in|mm|0|abbr=on}} chassis, with the cab positioned over the engine to give more load space. Export vehicles were the first Land Rovers to get the 2.6-litre petrol engine. Most examples had an [[E.N.V. Motor Syndicate|ENV]] (heavy duty) rear axle, a matching front axle came later. Tyres were large 900×16 types on deep-dish wheel rims to spread the ground weight of this heavy vehicle. These vehicles were somewhat underpowered for the increased load capacity ({{convert|1.5|LT|kg|abbr=on|disp=or}}), and most had a hard working life. Less than 2,500 were made, and most had a utility body, but surviving examples often have custom bodywork. With an upgraded powertrain, they can be used as a small motorhome. 

